{
  "id": "a1-polynomials-01",
  "version": 1,
  "title": "Exponent rules",
  "xp": 15,
  "tags": [
    "algebra",
    "polynomials"
  ],
  "learn": [
    {
      "id": "power",
      "kind": "text",
      "title": "What an exponent does",
      "body": "An exponent says how many times to multiply a base by itself: $x^3=x\\cdot x\\cdot x$. Three simple rules let you simplify without writing everything out."
    },
    {
      "id": "example-product",
      "kind": "example",
      "title": "Product rule",
      "body": "Same base, multiplying: add the exponents.",
      "steps": [
        "$x^2\\cdot x^3=(x\\cdot x)(x\\cdot x\\cdot x)=x^5$",
        "So $x^a\\cdot x^b=x^{a+b}$",
        "Numbers multiply as usual: $3x^2\\cdot4x^5=12x^7$"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "example-quotient",
      "kind": "example",
      "title": "Quotient rule",
      "body": "Same base, dividing: subtract the exponents.",
      "steps": [
        "$\\frac{x^5}{x^2}=\\frac{x\\cdot x\\cdot x\\cdot x\\cdot x}{x\\cdot x}=x^3$",
        "So $\\frac{x^a}{x^b}=x^{a-b}$"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "power-power",
      "kind": "text",
      "title": "Powers of powers",
      "body": "For $(x^3)^2$ you have $x^3$ twice, so $x^3\\cdot x^3=x^6$. In general $(x^a)^b=x^{ab}$. Also $(xy)^n=x^ny^n$, so $(2x)^3=8x^3$."
    },
    {
      "id": "not-adding",
      "kind": "tip",
      "body": "The rules need the same base. $x^2\\cdot y^3$ cannot be combined, and $x^2+x^3$ is not $x^5$."
    }
  ],
  "questions": [
    {
      "id": "q1",
      "type": "multiple_choice",
      "prompt": "Simplify $x^2\\cdot x^3$",
      "choices": [
        "$x^6$",
        "$x^5$",
        "$2x^5$"
      ],
      "answerIndex": 1,
      "explanation": "When you multiply powers with the same base, add the exponents: $2+3=5$."
    },
    {
      "id": "q2",
      "type": "type_answer",
      "prompt": "$2^3\\cdot2^2=2^n$. Find $n$.",
      "accepted": [
        "5",
        "5.0"
      ],
      "keypad": "numeric",
      "explanation": "Add exponents: $3+2=5$. Indeed $8\\cdot4=32=2^5$."
    },
    {
      "id": "q3",
      "type": "fill_blank",
      "prompt": "$\\frac{x^6}{x^2}=x^{\\_\\_}$",
      "choices": [
        "4",
        "8",
        "3"
      ],
      "answerIndex": 0,
      "explanation": "When dividing powers with the same base, subtract the exponents: $6-2=4$."
    },
    {
      "id": "q4",
      "type": "multiple_choice",
      "prompt": "Simplify $(x^3)^2$",
      "choices": [
        "$x^5$",
        "$x^6$",
        "$x^9$"
      ],
      "answerIndex": 1,
      "explanation": "A power of a power multiplies the exponents: $3\\cdot2=6$."
    },
    {
      "id": "q5",
      "type": "type_answer",
      "prompt": "$(2x)^3=ax^3$. Find $a$.",
      "accepted": [
        "8",
        "8.0"
      ],
      "keypad": "numeric",
      "explanation": "The power applies to both factors: $2^3x^3=8x^3$."
    },
    {
      "id": "q6",
      "type": "multiple_choice",
      "prompt": "Simplify $3x^2\\cdot4x^5$",
      "choices": [
        "$12x^7$",
        "$12x^{10}$",
        "$7x^7$"
      ],
      "answerIndex": 0,
      "explanation": "Multiply the numbers: $3\\cdot4=12$. Add the exponents: $2+5=7$."
    },
    {
      "id": "q7",
      "type": "match_pairs",
      "prompt": "Match each rule with an example.",
      "pairs": [
        [
          "product rule",
          "$x^2\\cdot x^3=x^5$"
        ],
        [
          "quotient rule",
          "$\\frac{x^5}{x^2}=x^3$"
        ],
        [
          "power rule",
          "$(x^2)^3=x^6$"
        ]
      ],
      "explanation": "Products add exponents, quotients subtract them, and powers of powers multiply them."
    },
    {
      "id": "q8",
      "type": "type_answer",
      "prompt": "$(x^2)^4\\cdot x=x^n$. Find $n$.",
      "accepted": [
        "9",
        "9.0"
      ],
      "keypad": "numeric",
      "explanation": "$(x^2)^4=x^8$, and $x=x^1$, so $x^8\\cdot x^1=x^9$."
    }
  ]
}
